.TH TERM 1
.SH NAME
term \- terminal for Plan 9 and POSIX programs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B term
[
.B -r
] [
.B -f
.I font.ttf
] [
.B -s
.I size
] [
.I cmd
[
.I arg ...
]
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Term
runs
.I cmd
.RB ( "rc -i"
by default) on a pseudo-terminal of
.IR ptyfs (4)
in its window of
.IR wsys (4)
or
.IR rio (1)
and shows what it writes as a terminal does: xterm's control
sequences, colours (16, 256 and 24-bit), bold, italic, underlined and
inverse text, the alternate screen of full-screen programs, and the
lines that went off the top (10000 of them).
It starts
.I ptyfs
and mounts it after
.B /dev
in a namespace of its own when there is none.
.PP
The program gets the pty's slave as
.B /dev/cons
and its
.I consctl
as
.BR /dev/consctl ,
in a namespace and note group of its own, so Plan 9's programs read
and write the console as in a window of
.IR rio ;
POSIX programs (APE) find a terminal with
.IR termios
and
.B TERM=xterm-256color.
The pty knows the window's size in rows and columns.
.PP
Text is drawn with a TrueType font,
.B /lib/font/ttf/Go-Mono.ttf
unless
.B -f
names another, its bold, italic and bold italic styles from the
files beside it
.RI ( name -Bold.ttf,
.IR name -Italic.ttf,
.IR name -Bold-Italic.ttf);
characters it lacks come from the other fonts in
.BR /lib/font/ttf ,
or are shown as an empty box.
.B -s
sets the height of a line in pixels (16).
The colours are the desktop's theme
.RB ( /dev/theme ,
.IR wsys (4):
its text window's text, background and selection, its menus; without
.I wsys
black on white), and so is the font
.RB ( monofont ),
unless
.B -f
names one;
.B -r
swaps text and background.
.PP
The mouse works as in
.IR rio :
button 1 selects text; button 2 holds a menu, as
.IR rio 's
without
.B cut
(what a terminal shows cannot be cut):
.B paste
(the snarf buffer typed, bracketed when the program asks),
.B snarf
(the selection into the snarf buffer),
.B plumb
(the selection, or the word where the menu was opened, to the
.IR plumber (4),
from the directory of the program on the pty),
.B look
(the selection's text, or that word, found further down the screen and
selected) and
.B send
(the selection, or the snarf buffer, typed with a newline at the end).
A click of button 3 plumbs the word under it, as in
.IR acme (1):
a file name with its line,
.BR file.c:42 ,
opens there.
The wheel and Shift with Page Up or Page Down scroll back; typing goes
back to the bottom.
A program that asked for the mouse gets it, unless Shift is held.
Where a shell reads lines (the pty in canonical mode) Del interrupts,
as in
.IR rio ;
in a full-screen program it is xterm's delete key.
Enter sends a carriage return and backspace DEL, as POSIX programs
expect.
A program's title becomes the window's label.
.I Term
ends when its program does or when its window is deleted, which hangs
the pty up.
.PP
With
.B TERMDEBUG=screen
in its environment
.I term
writes the text of its screen to
.BI /tmp/term. pid .screen
after each change and the cells' place to
.BI /tmp/term. pid .geom
.RI ( "x y width height rows columns" ),
for tests.
.SH EXAMPLES
A shell in a new window of
.IR wsys :
.IP
.EX
window term
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/cmd/term
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR vt (1),
.IR ptyfs (4),
.IR wsys (4),
.IR rio (1)
.SH BUGS
A program learns of a new size only when it asks: the pty posts no note
.RB ( SIGWINCH ).
There is no Alt (Meta) key: Plan 9's Alt composes characters.
